# OCB-CAL-03 — general 12x12 calibration polytope

Date: 2026-09-23

## Verdict

**PASS for a genuine 144-dimensional uncertainty set on the calibrated inverse map.**

Let `B0` be the nominal deconvolution map taking a conditioned recorded-destination vector `q` back
to an unnormalized true-destination score.  Consider the independently supplied convex uncertainty
box

`|B_jk-B0_jk| <= epsilon`

for all `144` entries.

For a candidate nearest neighbor `a` and competitor `b`, the score difference is

`(row_a(B)-row_b(B)) q`.

Because `q>=0` and `sum q=1`, the box can reduce the nominal margin by at most `2 epsilon`, and this
bound is attained by shifting every coefficient of the nearest row down and the competitor row up.

Across all twelve source preparations and every strict nearest-vs-nonnearest comparison, the exact
robustness threshold is

**epsilon_* = 0.09025558368189042**.

Below that value every map in the full 144D box yields the same strict top-two graph.  Above it, an
explicit perturbation reverses the limiting comparison.

A second control uses a rowwise `l1` uncertainty polytope

`||Delta B_j||_1 <= beta`.

Its exact all-source threshold is

**beta_* = 0.3279318016242833**.

## Scope

This is a robust-feasibility theorem for uncertainty in the **inverse calibration/deconvolution
operator**.  It is not identical to the nonlinear image of every physically admissible confusion
matrix plus destination-dependent efficiency vector.  Mapping a primitive detector-parameter
polytope into a certified `B` polytope remains a separate task.

## Decision

`OCB-CAL-03 = PASS_ON_INVERSE_CALIBRATION_POLYTOPE`.
